<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Thomas Henry Huxley</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1825">1825</a>–<a href="/years/1895">1895</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Darwin's Bulldog</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Thomas Henry Huxley was an English biologist and anthropologist, born in Ealing in 1825, who specialised in comparative anatomy and became known as Darwin's Bulldog for his advocacy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-darwin">Charles Darwin</a>'s theory of evolution. Virtually self-taught, he sailed as assistant surgeon on HMS Rattlesnake to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-guinea">New Guinea</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a> from 1846 and was elected a Fellow of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-society">Royal Society</a> on his return in 1850. His 1860 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a> debate with Samuel Wilberforce became a key moment in the acceptance of evolution, though some historians think parts of the surviving story are a later fabrication. He concluded that birds evolved from small carnivorous dinosaurs, coined the term agnosticism in 1869.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thomas-henry-huxley.jpg" alt="Thomas Henry Huxley" width="241" height="316" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Lock &amp; Whitfield · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:T.H.Huxley(Woodburytype).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Thomas Henry Huxley was born in Ealing, then a village in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middlesex">Middlesex</a>, the second youngest of eight children of George Huxley and Rachel Withers. His father was a mathematics schoolmaster at Great Ealing School until it closed, which put the family into financial difficulty, and Thomas left school at the age of ten after two years of formal schooling. Aged 20, too young to apply for a licence to practise from the Royal College of Surgeons and deep in debt, he applied at a friend's suggestion for an appointment in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-navy">Royal Navy</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After a lecture of 30 April 1852 he wrote that science in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> did everything but pay. He effectively resigned from the navy by refusing to return to active service and in July 1854 became professor of natural history at the Royal School of Mines. He was president of the Geological Society from 1868 to 1870 and of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-society">Royal Society</a> from 1883 to 1885. For nearly a decade he studied the relationship of man to the apes, which brought him into clash with Richard Owen, and in 1858 his Croonian Lecture rejected Owen's theory that the bones of the skull and the spine were homologous.</p><p>Huxley was at first not persuaded by development theory, as evolution was called, and he never quite said that Darwin was right about natural selection, being an empiricist who trusted what he could see. At the British Association meeting at the Oxford University Museum on Saturday 30 June 1860 he answered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of Oxford, Samuel Wilberforce, in the debate that followed a paper by John William Draper. Robert FitzRoy, captain of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hms-beagle">HMS Beagle</a>, spoke against Darwin. In November 1864 Huxley launched the X Club, a dining club of nine like-minded men including John Tyndall, J. D. Hooker and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herbert-spencer">Herbert Spencer</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Huxley was a pallbearer at the funeral of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-darwin">Charles Darwin</a> on 26 April 1882. In November 1870 he was elected to the London School Board, and his 1868 lecture On a Piece of Chalk presented science as organised common sense. When Huxley retired most universities had chairs in biological disciplines, where in his youth there were virtually no degrees in the biological sciences, and he was the single most influential person in that transformation. His Romanes Lecture of 1893 was Evolution and Ethics.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Thomas Henry Huxley” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The word Darwinism was coined by <b>Thomas Henry Huxley</b> in his March 1861 review of On the Origin of Species, and by the 1870s it described a range of concepts of evolution or development, without any specific commitment to Darwin's theory of natural selection.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/social-darwinism">Social Darwinism · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Human origins became central to the debate, although Darwin had avoided the subject in the book, and <b>Thomas Henry Huxley</b> argued it strongly.</blockquote><a href="/work/on-the-origin-of-species">On the Origin of Species · Reception</a></li></ul></section>
